//? Title: AC-3 on Sudoku: What Arc Consistency Can and Cannot Deduce
//? Subtitle: Constraint propagation with datamunge's algorithms::ac3 on Norvig's puzzle collections
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
*AC-3* (Mackworth, 1977) shrinks the domains of a constraint-satisfaction
problem by deleting every value that has no compatible partner in a
neighbour's domain, and repeats until nothing changes. We derive why its
result does not depend on the order in which arcs are processed, bound its
work, and show that for Sudoku's "different digits" constraints it is exactly
the familiar *naked single* rule. We then run it on all 156 puzzles of Peter
Norvig's three collections in `dm::datasets::sudoku()`. AC-3 alone solves
some easy puzzles outright, but on the hard ones it stalls with hundreds of
candidates left. Every table, number and figure is produced by the C\+\+
blocks in this document.
)

\toc

> The data

`dm::datasets::sudoku()` downloads (once) and parses Norvig's three puzzle
files: `easy50` (50 puzzles from Project Euler), `top95` (95 hard puzzles)
and `hardest` (11 puzzles collected as "the hardest"). Each row is one puzzle,
written as 81 characters read row by row, with `.` for a blank. The `givens`
column counts the filled cells.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  const auto puzzles = dm::datasets::sudoku();
  std::print("{} puzzles, columns {}.\n\n{}\n", puzzles.nrows(), puzzles.columns(),
             puzzles.head(3).to_md());
  std::print("\n{}\n",
             puzzles
               | dm::group_by("collection")
               | dm::summarise(agg::n("Puzzles"), agg::mean("givens", "Mean givens"),
                               agg::min("givens", "Min givens"),
                               agg::max("givens", "Max givens"))
               | dm::to_md(1));
}
```
