\caption(The first puzzles of the data, and the number of givens in each collection)

The hard collections give fewer digits. Below we see that the number of
givens predicts how far AC-3 gets quite well, though not completely.

> Sudoku as a constraint-satisfaction problem

A *binary constraint-satisfaction problem* (CSP) has variables
\(x_1,\dots,x_n\), a finite *domain* \(D_i\) of allowed values for each, and
constraints \(R_{ij}\subseteq D_i\times D_j\) between some pairs. A solution
assigns every \(x_i\) a value in \(D_i\) with \((x_i,x_j)\in R_{ij}\) for
every constraint. We store each constraint in both directions, as
\(R_{ij}\) and its converse \(R_{ji}=\{(b,a):(a,b)\in R_{ij}\}\), and call
an ordered pair \((i,j)\) an *arc*.

Sudoku has one variable per cell, so \(n=81\). A blank cell starts with
\(D_i=\{1,\dots,9\}\) and a given digit \(g\) with \(D_i=\{g\}\). Two cells are
*peers* if they share a row, column or \(3\times 3\) box. Each cell has
\(8+8+4=20\) peers: 8 in its row, 8 in its column and 4 more in its box.
Each pair of peers carries the constraint

$$ R_{ij}=\{(a,b): a\neq b\}, $$

so there are \(e=81\cdot 20=1620\) arcs. `sudoku::peers()` and
`sudoku::arcs()` build them, and `sudoku::board()` draws domains with
*pencil marks*: a single value as a large digit, and the surviving candidates
of other cells as small digits. The figure shows the first easy puzzle before
any deduction, with the centre cell and its peers shaded.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/ac3_peers.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sudoku = dm::algorithms::sudoku;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto puzzle = dm::datasets::sudoku().string_at("puzzle", 0);
  std::vector<int> shaded{40};
  std::ranges::copy(sudoku::peers(40), std::back_inserter(shaded));
  std::print("cell 40 has {} peers; {} arcs in all\n", shaded.size() - 1, sudoku::arcs().size());
  sudoku::board(sudoku::domains(puzzle), puzzle, shaded)
      .title("easy50 #1: cell 40 and its 20 peers")
      .save_svg("build/ac3_peers.svg");
}
```
