> Arc consistency and AC-3

An arc \((i,j)\) is *consistent* when every value in \(D_i\) has a *support*
in \(D_j\):

$$ \forall a\in D_i\;\exists b\in D_j:\;(a,b)\in R_{ij}. $$

A CSP is arc consistent when all of its arcs are. The basic step,
\(\mathrm{REVISE}(i,j)\), makes one arc consistent by deleting the
unsupported values:

$$ D_i \leftarrow \{a\in D_i : \exists b\in D_j,\ (a,b)\in R_{ij}\}. $$

AC-3 keeps a queue of arcs, initially all of them. It removes an arc from
the queue and revises it. If \(D_i\) shrank, it puts back every arc
\((k,i)\) with \(k\neq j\), because those values of \(x_k\) may have lost
their support in \(D_i\). It stops when the queue is empty, or reports
failure as soon as a domain becomes empty.

>> Soundness: no solution is lost

*Claim.* If \(s\) is a solution with \(s_v\in D_v\) for every \(v\), then
\(s_v\) stays in \(D_v\) throughout AC-3.

*Proof.* Consider one call \(\mathrm{REVISE}(i,j)\) made while
\(s_v\in D_v\) for all \(v\). Since \((s_i,s_j)\in R_{ij}\) and \(s_j\in D_j\),
the value \(b=s_j\) supports \(a=s_i\), so \(s_i\) is kept. Induction over
the sequence of revisions gives the claim. \(\square\)

So an empty domain proves the puzzle has no solution. If every domain ends
as a single value, those values are the solution: AC-3 never removes a
solution value, so the solution must be the only assignment left.

>> The result does not depend on the queue order

Call \(E=(E_1,\dots,E_n)\) a *sub-domain* of \(D\) if \(E_v\subseteq D_v\)
for every \(v\). The union of two arc-consistent sub-domains \(E\) and \(E'\)
is again arc consistent. A value \(a\in E_i\) has a support in
\(E_j\subseteq E_j\cup E'_j\), and likewise for \(a\in E'_i\). There are
finitely many sub-domains, so the union of all arc-consistent ones is itself
arc consistent. It is the unique *largest* arc-consistent sub-domain
\(D^\ast\).

*Claim.* AC-3 returns exactly \(D^\ast\), whatever order it takes arcs from
the queue.

*Proof.* First, AC-3 never removes a value of \(D^\ast\). The soundness
argument goes through with \(D^\ast\) in place of the single solution \(s\):
while \(D^\ast\subseteq D\), each \(a\in D^\ast_i\) has a support
\(b\in D^\ast_j\subseteq D_j\).

Second, the output is arc consistent. Consider the invariant *every arc not
in the queue is consistent*. It holds at the start, when every arc is
queued. After \(\mathrm{REVISE}(i,j)\), the arc \((i,j)\) is consistent.
Shrinking \(D_i\) cannot break an arc \((i,k)\), because the values to be
supported only got fewer. It can break an arc \((k,i)\) with \(k\neq j\),
and those are put back on the queue. The reverse arc \((j,i)\) cannot break.
If \(b\in D_j\) was supported by some \(a\in D_i\), then
\((a,b)\in R_{ij}\), so \(b\) also supports \(a\) and \(a\) survives.
This is why AC-3 may skip the reverse arc. So when the queue empties, the
domains are an arc-consistent sub-domain containing \(D^\ast\). Since
\(D^\ast\) is the largest, they equal \(D^\ast\). \(\square\)

>> Work bound

An arc \((k,i)\) is put back only when \(D_i\) loses a value, so at most
\(d=\max_v|D_v|\) times. Each arc is therefore revised at most \(d+1\) times.
One revision compares at most \(|D_i|\,|D_j|\le d^2\) pairs, giving the
classical bound of \(O(e\,d^3)\) compatibility checks.

>> What AC-3 means for Sudoku

For \(R_{ij}=\{a\neq b\}\), a value \(a\in D_i\) lacks support exactly when
every \(b\in D_j\) equals \(a\), that is, when \(D_j=\{a\}\) (for a non-empty
\(D_j\)). Hence

$$ \mathrm{REVISE}(i,j)\ \text{removes}\ a \iff D_j=\{a\}. $$

Arc consistency for Sudoku is therefore the *naked-single* rule: once a cell
is down to one digit, cross that digit off all its peers, and repeat.
Three exact consequences can be checked against the counters returned by
`ac3()` in the `Ac3Result`.

1. A revision removes at most one value. So the number of successful revisions equals the number of removed values \(R\), and the candidate total falls by exactly one at each step: \(C_{\text{end}}=C_0-R\), where \(C_0=9\,(81-\text{givens})+\text{givens}\).
2. Each removal at cell \(i\) re-queues at most the 19 arcs \((k,i)\) with \(k\neq j\). So the number of revisions is at most \(1620+19R\).
3. Checks stop at the first support, and the first two values of any \(D_j\) with \(|D_j|\ge 2\) cannot both equal \(a\). So a revision spends at most two checks per value of \(D_i\). If \(D_j\) is a single value, it spends one check per value of \(D_i\). Either way there are at most 18 checks per revision, far below the general \(d^2=81\).

The rule also shows what AC-3 *cannot* see. Suppose a digit fits in only
one cell of a row, but that cell still has other candidates. This is a
*hidden single*. It is a property of the nine-cell "all different"
constraint as a whole, and no single pair of cells can detect it. Binary
arc consistency therefore misses it.

> Running AC-3 on one puzzle

`sudoku::propagate()` runs `dm::algorithms::ac3()` on a puzzle's domains, its
1620 arcs and `sudoku::compatible()`. On the first easy puzzle, propagation
alone solves the grid, and `sudoku::is_solution()` confirms the result.
Digits deduced by AC-3 are drawn in blue.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/ac3_easy_solved.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sudoku = dm::algorithms::sudoku;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto puzzle = dm::datasets::sudoku().string_at("puzzle", 0);
  const auto r = sudoku::propagate(puzzle);
  const auto grid = sudoku::to_string(r.domains);
  std::print("consistent: {}, solved cells: {}/81\n", r.consistent, sudoku::solved_cells(r.domains));
  std::print("revisions {}, removals {}, checks {}\n", r.revisions, r.removals, r.checks);
  std::print("bounds: revisions <= 1620 + 19 * {} = {}, checks <= 18 * {} = {}\n", r.removals,
             1620 + 19 * r.removals, r.revisions, 18 * r.revisions);
  std::print("is_solution: {}\n", sudoku::is_solution(puzzle, grid));
  sudoku::board(r.domains, puzzle).title("easy50 #1 after AC-3: solved by propagation alone")
      .save_svg("build/ac3_easy_solved.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// consistent: true, solved cells: 81/81
// revisions 3591, removals 392, checks 10344
// bounds: revisions <= 1620 + 19 * 392 = 9068, checks <= 18 * 3591 = 64638
// is_solution: true
// \image(figures/ac3_easy_solved.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first easy puzzle after AC-3: givens in black, the 49 deduced digits in blue)
\alttext(
A completed Sudoku grid. The 32 given digits are black and the remaining 49
digits, all found by arc consistency, are blue.
)

The first puzzle of `top95` is different. AC-3 stops with most cells still
holding several candidates, although the puzzle has a unique solution. The
remaining pencil marks are exactly the arc-consistent domains \(D^\ast\).

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/ac3_hard_stalled.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sudoku = dm::algorithms::sudoku;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto puzzles = dm::datasets::sudoku()
                       | dm::filter_rows([](const auto& row) { return row.get_string("collection") == "top95"; });
  const auto puzzle = puzzles.string_at("puzzle", 0);
  const auto r = sudoku::propagate(puzzle);
  std::print("givens {}, solved cells after AC-3: {}/81, candidates left: {}\n",
             puzzles.double_at("givens", 0), sudoku::solved_cells(r.domains),
             sudoku::candidates(r.domains));
  sudoku::board(r.domains, puzzle).title("top95 #1 after AC-3: propagation stalls")
      .save_svg("build/ac3_hard_stalled.svg");
}
```
