> All 156 puzzles

`dm::mutate()` adds one column per AC-3 quantity to the puzzle frame, and a
grouped `summarise()` reduces them per collection. The last three columns
check the derivations above. *Bound slack* is \(1620+19R\) less the measured
revisions, so it must be non-negative, and *C rule* tests
\(C_{\text{end}}=C_0-R\). *Sound* checks soundness against an independent
exact solver. `sudoku::solve()` uses Knuth's Algorithm X, and
*Sound* is 1 when its solution lies inside the AC-3 domains.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sudoku = dm::algorithms::sudoku;
namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  const auto ac3 = [](const auto& row) { return sudoku::propagate(row.get_string("puzzle")); };
  const auto results =
      dm::datasets::sudoku()
      | dm::mutate("solved", [&](const auto& row) { return sudoku::solved_cells(ac3(row).domains) == 81 ? 1.0 : 0.0; })
      | dm::mutate("left", [&](const auto& row) { return double(sudoku::candidates(ac3(row).domains)); })
      | dm::mutate("revisions", [&](const auto& row) { return double(ac3(row).revisions); })
      | dm::mutate("checks", [&](const auto& row) { return double(ac3(row).checks); })
      | dm::mutate("slack", [&](const auto& row) {
          const auto r = ac3(row);
          return double(1620 + 19 * r.removals - r.revisions);
        })
      | dm::mutate("c_rule", [&](const auto& row) {
          const auto r = ac3(row);
          const double givens = row.get_double("givens");
          return 9 * (81 - givens) + givens - double(r.removals) == double(sudoku::candidates(r.domains)) ? 1.0 : 0.0;
        })
      | dm::mutate("sound", [](const auto& row) {
          const auto puzzle = row.get_string("puzzle");
          const auto domains = sudoku::propagate(puzzle).domains;
          const auto solution = sudoku::solve(puzzle);
          if (!solution || !sudoku::is_solution(puzzle, *solution)) return 0.0;
          for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c)
            if (!std::ranges::contains(domains[c], (*solution)[c] - '0')) return 0.0;
          return 1.0;
        });
  std::print("{}\n", results
                       | dm::group_by("collection")
                       | dm::summarise(agg::n("Puzzles"), agg::sum("solved", "Solved by AC-3"),
                                       agg::mean("left", "Candidates left"),
                                       agg::mean("revisions", "Revisions"),
                                       agg::mean("checks", "Checks"),
                                       agg::min("slack", "Bound slack (min)"),
                                       agg::min("c_rule", "C rule"), agg::min("sound", "Sound"))
                       | dm::to_md(1));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| collection | Puzzles | Solved by AC-3 | Candidates left | Revisions | Checks | Bound slack (min) | C rule | Sound |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| easy50 | 50 | 12 | 174.6 | 2566.5 | 9549.8 | 4771 | 1 | 1 |
| top95 | 95 | 0 | 282.8 | 2245.1 | 11447.8 | 4328 | 1 | 1 |
| hardest | 11 | 0 | 224.8 | 2422.6 | 10596.0 | 4800 | 1 | 1 |
// result_end
\caption(AC-3 per collection: puzzles solved outright, mean work, and checks of the derived identities; 1 in the last two columns means the check held for every puzzle in the collection)

Every puzzle satisfies both identities, and the exact solution always lies
inside the AC-3 domains, as soundness requires. The work is tiny next to
the general bound \(e\,d^3=1620\cdot 729\approx 1.2\times 10^6\) checks.
Propagation costs about \(10^4\) checks per puzzle, in line with the
Sudoku-specific limit of 18 checks per revision.

AC-3 solves 12 of the 50 easy puzzles, and none of the 106 hard ones. The figure
below traces the candidate total \(C\) after each successful revision. Each
step removes exactly one value, so every curve falls with slope \(-1\) and
stops at its fixpoint. Solved puzzles reach the floor \(C=81\). The others
level out well above it, at the point where no cell is left with a single
candidate whose removal from its peers would change anything.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/ac3_traces.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sudoku = dm::algorithms::sudoku;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto puzzles = dm::datasets::sudoku();
  const std::map<std::string, dm::RGB> colors{
      {"easy50", {37, 99, 235, 0.55}}, {"top95", {234, 88, 12, 0.45}}, {"hardest", {220, 38, 38, 0.9}}};
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < puzzles.nrows(); ++k) {
    const auto trace = sudoku::propagate(puzzles.string_at("puzzle", k)).candidates;
    std::vector<double> step(trace.size()), cand(trace.begin(), trace.end());
    std::iota(step.begin(), step.end(), 0.0);
    plot.lines(step, cand, colors.at(puzzles.string_at("collection", k)), 1.2);
  }
  plot.abline_h(81.0)
      .legend({"easy50", "top95", "hardest"}, {{37, 99, 235}, {234, 88, 12}, {220, 38, 38}})
      .title("Candidates remaining during AC-3, one line per puzzle")
      .x_label("successful revisions (= values removed)")
      .y_label("total candidates C")
      .size(820, 520)
      .save_svg("build/ac3_traces.svg");
}
```
