> A constraint where AC-3 decides everything

Sudoku's \(\neq\) constraints are about as weak as binary constraints get:
a value loses support only when a neighbour is already fixed. Order
constraints prune much more. Take five variables with domains
\(\{1,\dots,9\}\) and the chain \(x_0<x_1<x_2<x_3<x_4\). A value \(x_i=a\)
needs \(i\) smaller values below it and \(4-i\) larger values above it, so
any solution has \(i+1\le x_i\le 5+i\). Every \(a\) in that range is
supported by \(a-1\) on the left and by \(a+1\) on the right. So

$$ D^\ast_i=\{\,i+1,\dots,i+5\,\}, $$

which AC-3 finds through the general `ac3()` interface with a user-defined
constraint. Here arc consistency also guarantees a solution, because the
constraint graph is a path, which is a tree. For tree-structured CSPs, an
arc-consistent problem can be solved without backtracking: assign the
variables from the root outwards, always picking a supported value (Freuder,
1982). Sudoku's constraint graph is made of 27 overlapping nine-cell cliques,
so this guarantee does not apply.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const int n = 5;
  std::vector<std::vector<int>> domains(n, std::vector<int>{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9});
  std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> arcs;
  for (int i = 0; i + 1 < n; ++i) {
    arcs.emplace_back(i, i + 1);
    arcs.emplace_back(i + 1, i);
  }
  const auto less = [](int i, int a, int j, int b) { return i < j ? a < b : a > b; };
  const auto r = dm::algorithms::ac3(domains, arcs, less);
  std::print("| Variable | Domain after AC-3 | Predicted |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
    std::print("| x{} | {} | {}..{} |\n", i, r.domains[i], i + 1, i + 5);
  std::print("\nrevisions {}, removals {}, checks {}\n", r.revisions, r.removals, r.checks);
}
```
