| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| x0 | [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] | 1..5 |
| x1 | [2, 3, 4, 5, 6] | 2..6 |
| x2 | [3, 4, 5, 6, 7] | 3..7 |
| x3 | [4, 5, 6, 7, 8] | 4..8 |
| x4 | [5, 6, 7, 8, 9] | 5..9 |

revisions 14, removals 20, checks 431
// result_end
\caption(The ordering chain: every domain shrinks from nine values to the predicted five)

> Summary

- AC-3 computes the unique largest arc-consistent sub-domain. The result does not depend on the queue order, and it never loses a solution. All 156 puzzles confirm the soundness half.
- For Sudoku, arc consistency is exactly the naked-single rule. The measured counters match the derived identities \(C_{\text{end}}=C_0-R\) and \(\text{revisions}\le 1620+19R\) exactly.
- The rule solves only some easy puzzles, and stalls on every hard one. Hidden singles and harder patterns need either stronger propagation on the nine-cell all-different constraint or search. In practice AC-3 runs inside backtracking (*maintaining arc consistency*), where it prunes each branch of the search.

> References

- A. K. Mackworth (1977). Consistency in networks of relations. *Artificial Intelligence* 8(1), 99\-\-118.
- E. C. Freuder (1982). A sufficient condition for backtrack-free search. *Journal of the ACM* 29(1), 24\-\-32.
- P. Norvig. Solving every Sudoku puzzle. https://norvig.com/sudoku.html (source of the three collections).
- D. E. Knuth (2000). Dancing links. *Millennial Perspectives in Computer Science*, 187\-\-214.
